# Will the Prohibition Drama End?
Tamil title: மதுவிலக்கு நாடகம் ஒழியுமா?

Our leaders have spoken many times about the prohibition drama staged under Congress rule, and we too have written repeatedly about its futility.

The real idea behind Congress prohibition policy was a Brahminical political device: to find some pretext for destroying the people’s education, borrowing money, and imposing numerous taxes, and, after inflicting all this harm, deceiving the public into imagining that the rulers had accomplished something marvellous.

Furthermore, Congressmen gained votes by confusing and deceiving the people with lies and fraud. In the arrogance of their majority, and the dictatorial conceit that they could do anything they wished, they committed cruelties according to their whims. This prohibition scheme was one of them.

## Prohibition and Taxes

At that time they did not respect the opposition or heed its advice. They spoke arrogantly as though they would remain in office for a very long time, as though the country would always be governed according to their wishes, and as though they alone were responsible for every matter and were the experts capable of discovering and deciding everything. They danced as they pleased.

What is the result today? They have been driven or have fled from office. Interest continues to be paid on the debt incurred during their rule. The taxes they imposed continue to be collected. But the prohibition scheme created as the excuse for all these measures has alone become paralysed. Meanwhile liquor is smuggled and illicit arrack distilled; poor working people drink freely, spend more than before, and suffer.

Today is the Aadi Perukku festival. The number of people who have arrived in Salem, North Arcot, and Bhavani, ostensibly to take a sacred bath, may be in the hundreds of thousands. Some among them may indeed have come to immerse themselves in the Kaveri, but more than eighty out of every hundred have probably come to immerse themselves in toddy and arrack.

At a time of economic distress, when handicrafts have collapsed and workers suffer so severely that they must beg, people travel across one, two, or more districts and spend five or ten rupees on toddy or arrack that costs four or eight annas. We are compelled to ask whether this is the abolition of liquor or the abolition of poor people.

The Governor and his advisers came to power and reversed many harmful measures introduced under Congress rule. They removed Hindi, partly reduced the sales tax, made some changes to the tobacco law, restored educational fee concessions for working-class people that Congressmen had destroyed, stopped the wasteful salaries paid to members of the Legislative Assembly, and undertook several other measures.

When that is so, we ask why they should not immediately reverse Congress’s conspiracy and foolishness in the matter of prohibition and institute genuine prohibition honestly, in a manner that benefits the government, the public, farmers, and workers.

We believe our leaders will have conveyed this matter to the Viceroy as well. Congress prohibition in Bombay vanished because of a legal ruling. A similar legal question appears to have arisen at Vellore in this province. We write not to demand that prohibition be abandoned altogether, but to insist that it be administered in an orderly and useful manner, honestly and in good faith, without imposing excessive loss and hardship upon some or excessive profit and comfort upon others.

## What Has Prohibition Produced?

Present-day prohibition has not merely deprived the government administration of revenue; the amount drinkers spend continues to increase. Illegal activity connected with liquor also continues to grow. Because liquor is available elsewhere, some people have moved from one town to another. The condition of coconut cultivators and poor labourers formerly employed in the liquor trade has received no consideration. Without any relief, they suffer, wander, and struggle in the streets.

If this hardship had been distributed more or less equally across every district, people might find some route to resignation. Instead, prohibition exists in only four out of twenty-four districts. Workers and farmers in those districts look enviously toward the others and yearn. At the same time, taxes are imposed upon people in every other district to compensate for the losses caused by prohibition in those four. Those people lose heart and ask why their districts have no prohibition while they continue to pay its taxes.

After enduring so much hardship, loss, and trouble, if there is still no real prohibition, if hardened drinkers can still obtain liquor, and if everyone must suffer greater loss and hardship than before in the process, who can tolerate it?

We therefore request that the Madras government immediately suspend this district-by-district method of prohibition and introduce, as soon as possible, a suitable and effective system. We also warn that if it fails to do so, the public may be driven to conduct an intense agitation to abolish the prohibition conspiracy and cruelty created by Congress.

*Viduthalai*, 2 August 1940.

---NOTES---
The article distinguishes opposition to the existing implementation from opposition to prohibition itself. Historical currency values and administrative terms are retained. `ஆடி 18` is rendered as Aadi Perukku, the eighteenth day of the Tamil month Aadi associated with river observances.
